{% extends "base.html.twig" %}

{% set page_title = communication ? 'Communication' : 'Nouvelle communication' %}
{% block title %}{{ page_title }} — {{ site_name }}{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% include 'partials/page_header.html.twig' with { title: page_title } only %}

<div class="row"><div class="col-12 col-lg-8">
{# **No `data-submit-lock` here, and that is not an omission** (issue
   #706, IT-02). Publishing still waits on Meta for several seconds, and
   the second tap would still be a second public post — but from IT-02
   « Publier » is intercepted: the canvas is exported to a JPEG and
   attached to this form before it is posted. The shared lock cannot live
   with that. Its own deferred unlock reads `event.defaultPrevented` and
   GIVES THE BUTTON BACK when something prevented the default (see
   `form-submit-lock.js`, written that way in #756 for a different form),
   so opting in here would re-open the very double submit it exists to
   close, on the one page where a double submit is a second public post.

   `social-composer.js` holds the button itself instead, from the click
   that starts the export to the navigation that ends it, and
   `UxConventionsTest::testNoAsyncFormOptsIntoTheSubmitLock` now covers
   this template so the attribute cannot come back by accident. #}
<form method="post" action="{{ form_action }}" enctype="multipart/form-data" data-card-form>
    {{ csrf_field()|raw }}

    {# What is being shared, when this was opened from « Partager ». The
       source travels in the form rather than in the address: a composer
       has no address field at all, and the server describes this pair
       again, through the owning module, before writing anything. #}
    {% if from_source %}
        <input type="hidden" name="source_kind" value="{{ source_kind }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="source_id" value="{{ source_id }}">
        <p class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2 text-body-secondary mb-3">
            <i class="bi bi-images" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <span>Partage de {{ source_label }} <strong>{{ source.title|default('') }}</strong></span>
        </p>
    {% endif %}

    {# 1. The image, as it will be published — it is what is judged. #}
    <div class="mb-3" data-communication-image data-card-composer>
        {% if has_image and preview_path %}
            {# BOTH, on purpose (issue #706, IT-02). The image element
               below carries the card the server composes, and it is what a
               page whose JavaScript did not load — or a browser that gives
               no 2D context — still shows. `social-composer.js` draws the
               same card into the canvas and takes that element's place
               only once a draw has SUCCEEDED, so the page never ends up
               with a blank square where the card was. That matters here
               more than elsewhere:
               on a source-backed composer « Publier » is the only button,
               so a page showing no image would make the first POST an
               irreversible public post of something nobody saw. #}
            <img src="{{ preview_path }}" alt="Carte qui sera publiée : {{ source.title }}"
                 class="img-fluid rounded border" width="1080" height="1080" loading="lazy" data-card-preview>
            <canvas class="img-fluid rounded border" width="1080" height="1080" hidden
                    data-card-canvas
                    data-card-background="{{ background_path }}"
                    data-card-address="{{ card_address }}"
                    data-card-blur="{{ blur_ratio }}"
                    data-card-title="{{ source.title|default('') }}"
                    aria-label="Carte qui sera publiée : {{ source.title }}"></canvas>
            <p class="small text-body-secondary mt-2 mb-0">
                Voici exactement ce qui sera publié.
                {% if from_gallery %}Cette photo vient de la galerie : le curseur ci-dessous décide de son flou, partout où elle part.{% endif %}
            </p>
        {% elseif from_source and source and source.blockedReason %}
            {# `from_source` AND the reason, not the reason alone:
               `blockedReason` also carries « Donnez d'abord un titre à
               l'image. » for a plain communication, and this branch then
               swallowed its « Choisissez une image » prompt — the image
               box rendered nothing at all (raised in review).

               The source itself has gone — deleted, or no longer this
               chief's. Deliberately silent HERE: the reason is said once,
               in the alert before the destinations, and the branch below
               would send the visitor to an album that no longer exists to
               choose a cover photo in it. Both sentences used to appear
               together on this page, telling them to go and edit
               something it had just said was gone (raised in review). #}
        {% elseif from_source %}
            {# The source is there and has no image to compose a card from
               — an album without a cover photo. Said plainly, because
               « Publier » will refuse it and the reason belongs here, not
               in a flash message afterwards. #}
            <div class="alert alert-warning mb-0">
                {{ source_label|capitalize }} n'a pas d'image : aucune publication ne part sans image.
                Choisissez-en une là-bas, puis revenez.
            </div>
        {% else %}
            <div class="alert alert-secondary mb-0">Choisissez une image : aucune publication ne part sans image.</div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    {# 1b. The blur, as a slider (issue #706, IT-02).

       **Plain words, not a ratio**: « Net » to « Très flou ». The number
       behind it is a proportion of the image's short side, which is the
       right unit for the renderer and no unit at all for a chief.

       Offered only for a gallery photo, because an upload leaves exactly
       as it is and there is nothing to blur. Absent once something has
       left, like everything else about the card.

       There is NO floor any more. « Net » is net — measured, not assumed:
       `CardRenderer` used to clamp the radius up to one pixel, so a ratio
       of zero still cost a 1.4× loss of detail, and that is fixed in the
       same change. A slider whose left end does nothing is a lie told in
       an interface. #}
    {% if blur_adjustable|default(false) %}
        <div class="mb-3" data-card-blur-control>
            <label for="communication-blur" class="form-label small d-block">
                Flou de la photo
            </label>
            <input type="range" class="form-range" id="communication-blur" name="blur_ratio"
                   min="0" max="{{ blur_max }}" step="0.005" value="{{ blur_ratio }}"
                   data-card-blur-input
                   aria-describedby="communication-blur-help">
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-between small text-body-secondary">
                <span>Net</span>
                <span>Très flou</span>
            </div>
            {# The one sentence that matters, and it only appears when it
               is true: a gallery photo at « Net » leaves recognisable.
               Said before publishing rather than regretted after. #}
            <p class="small mt-2 mb-0" id="communication-blur-help">
                <span class="text-warning-emphasis" data-card-blur-sharp hidden>
                    <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    Cette photo de la galerie part sans flou.
                </span>
                <span class="text-body-secondary" data-card-blur-blurred hidden>
                    On devine l'ambiance, pas les visages. Pour voir les photos, il faut venir sur le site.
                </span>
            </p>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    {# 2. The two buttons that change it — absent when the image is the
       source's, which is the album's own to change, not this page's. #}
    {% if not frozen and uploadable %}
        <div class="row g-2 mb-2">
            {% if gallery_available %}
                <div class="col-6">
                    <button type="submit" name="action" value="gallery" class="btn btn-outline-secondary w-100">
                        <i class="bi bi-images" aria-hidden="true"></i> Galerie
                    </button>
                </div>
            {% endif %}
            <div class="{{ gallery_available ? 'col-6' : 'col-12' }}">
                {# **One click** (issue #706, IT-02). Without JavaScript this
                   stays what it was: choose a file in the field below, then
                   press this button — two steps, and the field has to be
                   visible for the first. With JavaScript,
                   `social-composer.js` hides the field, makes this button
                   open the picker, and submits as soon as a file is chosen.
                   The button keeps `type="submit"` and its `name`/`value`
                   so the no-JS path is the one the markup describes. #}
                <button type="submit" name="action" value="upload" class="btn btn-outline-secondary w-100"
                        data-card-upload-button>
                    <i class="bi bi-upload" aria-hidden="true"></i> Téléverser
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="mb-2" data-card-upload-field>
            <label for="communication-image" class="form-label small">Image à téléverser</label>
            <input type="file" class="form-control form-control-sm" id="communication-image" name="image"
                   accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp" data-card-upload-input>
        </div>
        <p class="small text-body-secondary mb-4">
            Une image téléversée part telle quelle. Une image de la galerie part au flou que vous choisissez,
            le même vers toutes les destinations.
        </p>
    {% elseif frozen and from_source %}
        {# What is true for a source-backed share, which is less than the
           message below promises: the TEXT is frozen — every later
           destination receives the caption the first one got — but the
           title and the image are read from the album or the article at
           each publication, including a retry. Rename the album between a
           successful Facebook post and an Instagram retry and the two
           destinations get different cards. Promising otherwise here also
           contradicted the title field's own help a few lines below,
           which says the title is changed at the source. Raised in review
           on the pull request for IT-01. The image half is what IT-02
           settles, by storing the image the browser composed and serving
           that one to every later attempt; the title stays the source's
           by design — nothing of the source is copied into the
           communication (specifications.md §4). #}
        <div class="alert alert-info small mb-4">
            Cette communication est déjà partie vers une destination : le texte ne change plus, pour que
            chaque destination reçoive le même message. Le titre et l'image restent ceux de
            {{ source_label }} : s'ils y changent, une nouvelle tentative partira avec les nouveaux.
        </div>
    {% elseif frozen %}
        <div class="alert alert-info small mb-4">
            Cette communication est déjà partie vers une destination : l'image et le texte ne changent plus,
            pour que chaque destination reçoive la même chose.
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    {# 3. The title written on the image. Read-only and taken from the
       source when there is one: an album's name is the album's. #}
    {% if from_source %}
        {% include 'partials/form_field.html.twig' with {
            field_id: 'communication-title',
            label: 'Titre sur l\'image',
            value: source.title|default(''),
            readonly: true,
            help: 'Le titre est celui de ' ~ source_label ~ ' : il se change là-bas.',
        } only %}
    {% else %}
        {% include 'partials/form_field.html.twig' with {
            field_id: 'communication-title',
            field_name: 'title',
            label: 'Titre sur l\'image',
            value: communication.title|default(''),
            maxlength: title_max,
            readonly: frozen,
        } only %}
    {% endif %}

    {# 4. The text of the post. #}
    {% include 'partials/form_field.html.twig' with {
        field_id: 'communication-body',
        field_name: 'body',
        label: 'Texte de la publication',
        type: 'textarea',
        rows: 4,
        value: communication.body|default(source.defaultCaption|default('')),
        maxlength: body_max,
        readonly: frozen,
        help: 'Sur Instagram, une adresse en légende n\'est pas cliquable : c\'est pour ça qu\'elle figure sur l\'image.',
    } only %}

    {# Where the card the browser drew travels (issue #706, IT-02).

       A file input rather than a base64 string in a hidden field: a
       1080² JPEG is a few hundred kilobytes, and base64 would add a third
       to that and arrive as a POST value the server would have to decode
       before it could check anything. `social-composer.js` fills this
       through `DataTransfer`, as `finance-receipt-form.js` already does
       for a downscaled photo.

       Hidden rather than absent on a frozen share: the controller
       ignores a card once something has left, and a field that vanished
       would make the page's shape depend on state for no reason. #}
    <label for="communication-card" class="d-none">Carte composée</label>
    <input type="file" name="card" id="communication-card" accept="image/jpeg"
           class="d-none" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" data-card-file>

    {# 5. The destinations, then publishing. #}
    {% if source and source.blockedReason %}
        <div class="alert alert-warning">{{ source.blockedReason }}</div>
    {% endif %}
    {% include '@social/share/_destinations.html.twig' with { destinations: destinations, offers_groups: offers_groups|default(false), groups: groups|default([]), csp_nonce: csp_nonce|default('') } only %}

    <div class="alert alert-warning small" data-publish-warning>
        Sur Facebook et Instagram, une fois publié, c'est <strong>public</strong> et hors du site : personne ne pourra le reprendre d'ici.
        {% if offers_groups|default(false) %}Dans un groupe de discussion, la publication reste dans le site et suit les règles du groupe.{% endif %}
    </div>

    {# « Donnez d'abord un titre » — computed on the TYPING (issue #706,
       IT-02).

       The server computes the same refusal from the SAVED title
       (`ShareSourceResolver::communication()`), which is right and is
       still the real guard — but on its own it meant the block stayed up
       while the chief was typing the very title that lifts it, and only
       cleared after a publication attempt that was refused. Rendered
       hidden and shown by `social-composer.js`, so a page with no
       JavaScript simply keeps the server's behaviour.

       `aria-live` because it appears while the field is being typed in,
       with the focus elsewhere than here. #}
    {% if not frozen and not from_source %}
        <p class="small text-warning-emphasis mb-2 text-end" data-card-title-missing hidden
           aria-live="polite">
            <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            Donnez d'abord un titre à l'image.
        </p>
    {% endif %}

    {# No « Enregistrer »: « Publier » is what saves (IT-01). #}
    <div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2 justify-content-end align-items-center">
        <a class="btn btn-link" href="/medias-sociaux">Voir l'historique</a>
        <button type="submit" name="action" value="publish" class="btn btn-primary"
                data-confirm="Publier maintenant ? Sur Facebook et Instagram, c'est public et hors du site : personne ne pourra le reprendre d'ici."
                data-confirm-label="Publier">
            <i class="bi bi-share" aria-hidden="true"></i> Publier
        </button>
    </div>
</form>
</div></div>
{% endblock %}

{% block scripts %}
    {# The card drawn in the browser (issue #706, IT-02). The engine first:
       `social-composer.js` reads `window.ScoutMagicCard` at load and does
       nothing at all without it, so the order is what makes the canvas
       appear rather than an error in the console. #}
    <script src="{{ asset('/assets/js/social-card.js') }}" defer></script>
    <script src="{{ asset('/assets/js/social-composer.js') }}" defer></script>
{% endblock %}
